window.HUB_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","t":"AI Skills and agents › AI Skills › Case study Case study · How skills are built What Anthropic’s open legal skills teach us about building AI workflows — and why this does not make Harvey or Legora obsolete. Start reading The repository on GitHub ↗ Back to the AI skills → Subject Anthropic’s Claude for Legal project, and its ai-governance-legal plugin Licence of the source Apache License 2.0 Written for Anyone building or evaluating an AI skill — no coding background assumed Reading time About 25 minutes An independent case study. This is an educational case study of an open-source project. It is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by Anthropic, Harvey, or Legora. It simplifies and adapts concepts for teaching: the code samples below are teaching illustrations, not verbatim reproductions of Anthropic’s files. Read the repository itself for the real thing. The objective is not to reproduce Anthropic’s work line by line. Instead, we are going to take it apart like engineers examining a machine: What is a skill? What does the .md file actually do? Why was the workflow divided this way? What belongs in the skill and what belongs elsewhere? And how is this different from buying a legal AI platform such as Harvey or Legora? Or listen · audio explainer A spoken walkthrough of the same ground, if you would rather hear it than read it. The written version below is the fuller one — it carries the diagrams, the file listings, and the checklist. 21 min 55 sec Open in Google Drive ↗ Contents Skill or platform? sections 1–4 What a SKILL.md file actually is sections 5–14 Design lessons from the plugin sections 15–23 Back to the market, and a checklist sections 24–28 The source Read the repository yourself Everything above is a reading of a public, Apache-2.0 licensed project: Anthropic’s Claude for Legal, and the ai-governance-legal plugin inside it. Every skill discussed here is a plain Markdown file you can open in a browser. Go and look at one. anthropics/claude-for-legal ↗ Independent case study. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Anthropic. ← The AI skills How to install a skill → Robert Crown Law Library · last reviewed August 2026."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#part-1","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Skill or platform?","t":"Part one The question every lawyer asks first, and why the answer is not a winner-takes-all one."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s1","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Start with the question everyone asks","t":"1 Section one When lawyers see something like Anthropic’s Claude for Legal project, an understandable reaction is: “If Claude can do legal work and Anthropic is giving away legal skills, why aren’t Harvey, Legora, and similar legal AI companies suddenly out of business?” The short answer: because a skill is not the same thing as a complete legal AI platform. And even Claude itself is not merely a model any more. The boundaries are converging. That is what makes this interesting. Anthropic describes legal AI as an ecosystem that can include connectors to firm data, reusable skills, subagents, plugins, APIs, and enterprise controls. In Anthropic’s own explanation, connectors provide access to data, skills teach Claude how to perform repeatable legal tasks, and plugins bundle capabilities for particular practice areas. Harvey and Legora are approaching much of the same problem from another direction: they provide legal-specific environments that package many of these capabilities into a finished product. Harvey currently combines purpose-built agents, legal research, document storage and bulk analysis through Vault, collaboration, institutional knowledge, workflow orchestration, integrations, analytics, and enterprise controls. Legora similarly combines agentic execution with workflows, drafting, review, legal research, database search, firm knowledge, permissions, and legal-specific tools. So this is not Claude Legal versus Harvey and Legora, winner takes all. A better picture is a stack, and companies can participate at several layers at once — which, increasingly, they do. Legal work experience matters · collaboration · Word · review Legal applications research · vaults · diligence · contracts Workflows, agents, and skills “how should this legal task be done?” Firm knowledge and external data precedents · matters · research sources Connectors, permissions, governance who may reach what, and on whose authority Foundation models Claude · GPT · other models"},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s2","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"So why aren’t Harvey and Legora “out of business”?","t":"2 Section two Because having an intelligent model plus a workflow definition is not the entire product problem. Consider what Harvey currently provides. Its platform includes agents, research, Vault, knowledge resources, Shared Spaces, workflows, contract intelligence, integrations, administrative capabilities, and enterprise security features. Harvey’s Vault can organise and analyse large document collections and connect with document-management systems while maintaining permissions and governance controls. Legora’s Workflows similarly lets lawyers orchestrate legal tasks using drafting, tabular review, legal research, database search, institutional standards, reference materials, and access controls. That productisation matters. Imagine that a law firm wants an AI system for due diligence. The AI reasoning might be only one portion of the problem. You also need to answer: Where are the 15,000 documents? Who may access them? What matter do they belong to? Which precedents should be used? What research sources are licensed? Who can see the output? Can the system analyse them in bulk? How are citations checked? Where does the final work product live? Can lawyers collaborate on it? Does it work inside Word? Can administrators control access? Can the firm audit usage? How is the system rolled out to 3,000 lawyers? Who supports it when something breaks? A SKILL.md file does not magically solve all of those problems."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s3","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"A useful mental model: capability versus product","t":"3 Section three Skill: here is how to perform this job. Platform: here is the environment in which thousands of people can actually perform this work."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s3","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"gives the AI a playbook","t":"A skill “When someone proposes a new AI use case, first determine what the system does. Check our registry. Check prohibited uses. Determine the governance tier. Identify required reviews. Produce the result in this format.” That is enormously valuable. But it is primarily a behavioural specification . It tells an AI system: here is how we want this particular job performed."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s3","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"packages the surrounding environment","t":"A platform Interfaces lawyers already understand, legal research sources, document repositories, matter organisation, integrations, permissions, collaboration, institutional knowledge, workflow builders, analytics, auditability, deployment support, security controls, vendor support, and preconfigured legal experiences. Harvey’s current platform demonstrates this broader product approach, and Legora likewise describes its platform as connecting legal tools and workflows into a broader environment for legal work."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s4","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"But the distinction is getting blurrier","t":"4 Section four This is important. It would be misleading to teach that “Claude is just the model and Harvey is the application.” That description is becoming outdated. Anthropic now describes a legal ecosystem involving connectors, skills, plugins, subagents, APIs, enterprise controls, and workplace integrations — not merely access to a foundation model. At the same time, Harvey and Legora increasingly expose configurable agents and workflows rather than only fixed software features. Harvey now describes purpose-built agents that can execute legal work, while Legora explicitly offers both agentic Workflows and reusable Skills within its environment. More packaged More configurable Harvey and Legora workflows Claude plus skills and plugins Except even that is oversimplified. Each company is moving in both directions. The more useful competitive question is becoming: Who provides the best combination of intelligence, workflow design, legal data, institutional knowledge, integration, user experience, governance, reliability, and deployment? That is a much more interesting question than simply asking whose underlying model is smartest."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#part-2","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"What a SKILL.md file actually is","t":"Part two Open the machine. A skill file has exactly two parts, and neither of them is code."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s5","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Now examine Anthropic’s AI governance legal plugin","t":"5 Section five The repository itself provides an important clue about how Anthropic thinks about the architecture. Its repository guidance describes the project as primarily involving prompt content — skills, agents, hooks, plugin metadata, and configuration — rather than conventional application code. The repository layout identifies individual skill entrypoints at a predictable path, alongside plugin configuration, connectors, agents, and other components: the shape of a plugin ai-governance-legal/ .claude-plugin/ # plugin metadata CLAUDE.md # the practice profile the skills read references/ # knowledge the skills consult skills/ <name> /SKILL.md # one skill, one job And AI Governance Legal is not one enormous skill. It contains separate jobs, each with its own folder and its own SKILL.md : cold-start-interview customize ai-inventory use-case-triage aia-generation vendor-ai-review reg-gap-analysis policy-monitor policy-starter matter-workspace A terminology note. AIA means AI impact assessment, so aia-generation means, essentially, “generate an AI impact assessment.” That unusual-looking name is intentional, not a typo: it is the actual skill and directory name in the repository. The use-case-triage skill handles one job: triaging a proposed AI use and determining the governance path. The aia-generation skill owns another job: conducting a more structured AI impact assessment, including intake, risk analysis, regulatory classification, policy comparison, and recommendations. That separation is intentional. And it teaches us our first major skill-design lesson: A sophisticated AI system is often better designed as several small skills than as one enormous prompt."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s6","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"What exactly is a SKILL.md file?","t":"6 Section six Let’s demystify the filename. .md simply means Markdown , a way of writing formatted documents using ordinary text: markdown # Big heading ## Smaller heading - bullet - bullet **bold text** You do not need special software to understand a Markdown file. It opens in VS Code, Notepad, TextEdit, GitHub, or almost any plain-text editor. There is no mysterious AI programming language hidden inside it. Anthropic itself describes simple skills as Markdown-based workflows; more advanced skills can also include executable scripts. A simplified skill might look something like this: SKILL.md — teaching illustration --- name : use-case-triage description : > Classify proposed AI use cases and identify governance requirements and next steps. --- # Purpose Determine the governance path for a proposed AI use. # Workflow 1. Understand the proposed use. 2. Check existing policy. 3. Check prohibited uses. 4. Determine the risk tier. 5. Identify required reviews. 6. Produce a structured result. # Output Include: - classification - rationale - required conditions - responsible owner - next action # Boundaries Do not treat the assessment as final organizational approval. A teaching simplification, not a verbatim reproduction of Anthropic’s file — but it demonstrates the basic anatomy."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s7","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Anatomy of the file","t":"7 Section seven A SKILL.md file contains two different kinds of information. 1. YAML frontmatter A small “label card” for the skill: what is this skill, and when might the system need it? 2. Markdown body The instructions for doing the job: now that you have chosen this skill, here is how to perform it. If those terms sound technical, don’t worry. The easiest way to think about them is that YAML tells the system what the skill is , and Markdown tells the system how the skill works . Let’s unpack that one piece at a time."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s8","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"First: what is YAML?","t":"8 Section eight YAML is a simple way of writing structured information in plain text. That sounds much more technical than it really is. Imagine the label on a file folder: a name, and a purpose. YAML lets us express that same kind of information in a predictable format that software can recognise: yaml name : ai-project-triage description : Classify proposed AI projects and identify required governance steps. The colon separates the name of the field from the information stored in it — label: value . For the simple YAML used in a skill, you do not need to learn a programming language."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s9","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"So what is “frontmatter”?","t":"9 Section nine Frontmatter is a small block of information placed at the very beginning of a document. Think of it as the information printed on the outside of the folder before you open it. In a skill, it might look like this: the frontmatter block --- ← start of frontmatter name : ai-project-triage description : > Classify proposed organizational AI projects and identify required governance steps. Use when someone asks whether an AI use is permitted, prohibited, or requires additional review. --- ← end of frontmatter After that comes the regular Markdown document containing the workflow."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s10","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"And what does “metadata” mean?","t":"10 Section ten You may also hear the information in that top section described as metadata . That is another technical-sounding word with a simple meaning: metadata is information about something. A book contains the actual story. Information about the book is its title, author, and publication year. A photograph has an image; information about the photograph might be the date taken, the camera, the location, and the file size. Likewise, the YAML at the top of our skill contains information about the skill. The actual instructions for performing the work come later."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s11","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"What does the > mean?","t":"11 Section eleven You may notice description: > and wonder why that little > is there. It tells YAML that the description continues onto the indented lines below. So a description broken across four lines is still one description — it is simply set out so that a human can read it more comfortably. You do not need to memorise YAML syntax to design a skill. The important point is understanding why this information exists ."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s12","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Why does that tiny description matter so much?","t":"12 Section twelve Before the AI follows a skill’s detailed instructions, the system first has to decide: is this the right skill for this request? A skill’s metadata — especially its name and description — helps determine whether the skill should be selected. Once selected, the detailed instructions in SKILL.md become available. This is why a vague description creates problems. description: Helps with AI governance. sounds reasonable to a human, but it gives the system very little help: almost anything could qualify as “AI governance.” Compare: a description that can be matched description : > Classify proposed organizational AI uses as approved, conditional, prohibited, or requiring further review. Use when a user asks whether an AI system may be used, what approvals are necessary, or what governance process applies. Now the system has recognisable signals: A user asks: “Can we use this AI tool?” · “Does this project need approval?” · “What review does this AI system need?” The description matches those words Consider the AI project triage skill The YAML description is the sign on the specialist’s door. It tells the system: this specialist handles these kinds of problems."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s13","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Think of the description as the receptionist","t":"13 Section thirteen Imagine an organisation with ten specialists. Someone walks through the door and says: “Marketing wants to upload customer interviews into an AI transcription service. Can we do that?” Before anyone performs the work, somebody has to decide where the question belongs . AI inventory? No. Policy drafting? No. Impact assessment? Maybe later. Vendor AI review? Possibly later. Use-case triage? Yes. That routing decision happens before the detailed workflow begins. Descriptions are not merely marketing copy. They help route work to the correct skill. Which gives us a useful distinction: the description answers “what kinds of problems belong here?”, and the skill instructions answer “now that the problem is here, how should we solve it?”"},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s14","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Part two of the file: the Markdown instructions","t":"14 Section fourteen Once the skill has been selected, the Markdown body tells the AI how to perform the job. the body of a skill # Purpose Provide fast, consistent governance triage. ## Step 1 — Understand the use case Determine: - what the AI does - who is affected - what data is involved - whether human review exists ## Step 2 — Check policy Read `references/governance-policy.md`. ## Step 3 — Check prohibitions If a prohibited use applies, stop and explain why. ## Step 4 — Classify Choose: - Approved - Conditional - Prohibited - Insufficient information ## Step 5 — Produce the output Use the required output template. Now the file is behaving almost like a small program. Not computer code exactly — but it has an input, a procedure, conditions, branches, and an output. A well-designed skill is a small program written partly in natural language."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#part-3","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Design lessons from the plugin","t":"Part three Where a job ends, what stays out of the file, and what changes from one organisation to the next."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s15","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Forensics on Anthropic’s triage skill","t":"15 Section fifteen The use-case-triage skill handles the kind of hallway question that organisations constantly receive: “Can we just use AI for this?” Instead of answering that question informally every time, the skill turns it into a repeatable governance workflow. Conceptually, the process looks something like this: A request arrives Understand the use case Consult organisational context Check the use-case registry Check the red lines Determine a classification approved conditional not approved Identify requirements Route the next action The skill does not try to own every possible step after triage. If deeper review is necessary, another workflow can take over. That is excellent skill design, and it gives us another principle: A skill should know both what job it owns and where that job ends."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s16","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Why not put everything into one SKILL.md?","t":"16 Section sixteen Why not put everything into one SKILL.md ? Because then you get the mega skill: AI governance and privacy and contract review and impact assessments and vendor review and regulatory research and policy drafting and litigation and employment and IP and research, all in one file. At first this seems convenient — “let’s create one legal skill that knows everything!” But several problems appear at once. The instruction file becomes enormous. The AI has more irrelevant instructions competing for attention. Routing becomes fuzzy. Testing becomes difficult. Updates become risky. Responsibility between workflows becomes unclear. Instead, a small router hands off to a triage skill, an assessment skill, a vendor skill, and a policy skill — each with a recognisable job. That modularity is one of the strongest lessons from this case."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s17","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"SKILL.md should be a control plane, not a textbook","t":"17 Section seventeen A common beginner instinct is: “I have a 70-page AI policy. I’ll paste the entire thing into SKILL.md .” Don’t. The skill should contain the instructions necessary to run the workflow. Large bodies of information that are needed only in particular situations can live in supporting resources: one skill folder ai-project-triage/ │ ├── SKILL.md │ ├── references/ │ ├── governance-policy.md │ ├── prohibited-uses.md │ └── escalation-rules.md │ ├── scripts/ │ └── validate-output.py │ └── assets/ └── assessment-template.docx The SKILL.md might simply say: before classifying a use case, read references/prohibited-uses.md ; if the case requires escalation, consult references/escalation-rules.md . This progressive structure is intentional: metadata helps determine whether the skill applies, SKILL.md provides the essential workflow, and supporting resources can be consulted when they become relevant. SKILL.md is the control panel, not the warehouse."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s18","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Four different things beginners often mix together","t":"18 Section eighteen SKILL.md — procedure How to perform a job: check policy, identify risks, classify, produce this output. references/ — knowledge The information needed to perform the job: here is our actual policy. scripts/ — deterministic computation Code for operations that must be exact: validate these 5,000 records against the required schema. assets/ — reusable output material Things used in the final deliverable: use this Word template for the report. Don’t make SKILL.md carry all four jobs."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s19","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Another forensic clue: configuration is separated from workflow","t":"19 Section nineteen Anthropic’s AI Governance Legal design also separates organisational configuration from the generic workflow. This is particularly clear in the aia-generation skill — again, AIA means AI impact assessment. Anthropic describes this skill as conducting a structured AI impact assessment, including risk analysis, regulatory classification, policy comparison, and recommendations. It also draws on an organisation’s configured “house style” rather than assuming every organisation conducts an assessment identically. Skill I know how to conduct an AI impact assessment. + Organisation profile Here is how our organisation evaluates and documents risk. + Current matter Here is the AI system we are assessing. = Result An organisation-specific AI impact assessment. This is a reusable pattern worth stealing. The skill contains the method . The organisation supplies the configuration . The current matter supplies the facts ."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s20","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Build an onboarding skill when configuration matters","t":"20 Section twenty Anthropic includes a cold-start-interview capability for a reason. Before all of the other workflows can consistently reflect an organisation, something has to establish the organisation’s working context: its risk posture, policies, jurisdictions, escalation contacts, approved and prohibited uses, assessment format, vendor positions, and sign-off process. Anthropic’s quick-start materials describe these setup interviews as creating a practice profile that other skills can then read: Setup interview An organisational profile is written down Every other skill reads it triage impact assessment vendor review That is much better than teaching every individual skill the organisation’s entire history. And it reveals another powerful design pattern: Sometimes the first skill you need is not a task skill. It is a configuration skill."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s21","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"The three-layer rule for designing your own skill","t":"21 Section twenty-one Layer 1 — Trigger When should this skill run? Put that primarily in the description. A user says “Can we use AI to screen applicants?” and the triage skill should be the one that answers. Layer 2 — Workflow What sequence should the AI follow? Put that in SKILL.md : understand the use, check policy, check red lines, classify, identify conditions, produce the output. Layer 3 — Resources What does the AI need to consult or use? Put those in supporting files or connected systems: policy, regulations, templates, schemas, scripts, examples. Which gives us: trigger → skill → resources → output."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s22","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"A teaching template for a good skill","t":"22 Section twenty-two Here is a simplified example you could use as a starting point. ai-project-triage/SKILL.md --- name: ai-project-triage description: > Classify proposed organizational AI projects and identify required governance steps. Use when a user asks whether an AI use is permitted, what approvals are required, or whether an additional privacy, security, legal, or impact review is necessary. --- # Objective Determine the appropriate governance path for a proposed AI use. # Required context Before classification, determine: - What will the AI do? - Who is affected? - What data will it process? - What happens because of its output? - Is there human review? - Which vendor or model is involved? # Workflow 1. Understand the use case. 2. Read `references/governance-policy.md`. 3. Check prohibited uses first. 4. Check previously approved use cases. 5. Determine governance tier. 6. Identify required reviews. 7. Produce the required output. # Stop conditions If essential information is missing: - gather only the missing information. If a prohibited-use rule applies: - stop the ordinary workflow; - identify the controlling policy; - explain the escalation path. # Output ## Use case [one-sentence summary] ## Classification [Approved / Conditional / Prohibited / Insufficient Information] ## Basis [short explanation] ## Required actions | Action | Owner | |---|---| ## Next step [recommended workflow] # Boundaries Do not represent the classification as final organizational approval when human sign-off is required. The interesting part isn’t the prose. It’s the architecture: trigger, context, procedure, branching, stop condition, output contract, boundary. Those are the building blocks."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s23","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Notice what we deliberately left out","t":"23 Section twenty-three We did not put these into the skill: the complete EU AI Act, the entire company AI policy, 600 approved vendors, 50 historical impact assessments, the security architecture, all of privacy law, or every output example. Those things might still be useful. But they belong in the appropriate place — reference files, connected data systems, research tools, databases, other skills, or external applications. Which leads to one of the most useful rules in skill design: Put the procedure in the skill. Put the library in the library."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#part-4","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"Back to the market, and a checklist","t":"Part four What an open skill proves, what it does not, and twelve questions to ask of any skill you meet."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s24","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"And this brings us back to Harvey and Legora","t":"24 Section twenty-four Now we can answer the original question more precisely. A sophisticated SKILL.md can encode something valuable: institutional legal procedure . That is significant. It means firms can increasingly express portions of their expertise as reusable AI workflows. But legal AI platforms are selling considerably more than a prompt or a Markdown file. They are packaging combinations of: models legal workflows legal data institutional knowledge document infrastructure matter context research collaboration integrations permissions governance administration user experience deployment support Harvey’s current offering demonstrates this kind of bundled approach, combining agents, research, document infrastructure, knowledge resources, collaboration, workflows, integrations, and enterprise controls. Legora similarly combines agents and workflows with research, drafting, review, database search, institutional materials, permissions, and other legal tools. Meanwhile, Anthropic is moving upward from the foundation-model layer through skills, plugins, connectors, subagents, workplace integrations, APIs, and enterprise infrastructure. So there is real competitive overlap. But: An open legal skill demonstrates that legal workflow knowledge can be encoded. It does not demonstrate that every other layer of a legal technology platform has become unnecessary."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s25","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"In fact, skills may make legal platforms more interesting","t":"25 Section twenty-five There is another possible outcome. Skills could increasingly become a kind of workflow layer through which organisations express how they want AI-assisted work performed. Imagine a firm develops its own our-nda-playbook , our-due-diligence-method , our-ai-governance-triage , our-privacy-review , and our-client-alert-style . The strategically important asset may increasingly become the firm’s encoded way of working rather than simply the prompt box through which it accesses a model. Then the platform question becomes: where can our institutional workflows run best? That might ultimately involve Claude, ChatGPT, Harvey, Legora, an internal application, or some combination. This is an inference about where the architecture could lead, not a claim that today’s products already offer interchangeable or fully portable skills. In fact, Legora now also uses the term Skills for reusable instructions that encode a firm’s processes and standards inside its own agent environment. That convergence makes the underlying idea even more important. The terminology and file formats may differ; the architectural question remains: How do we encode institutional expertise so that an AI can apply it consistently?"},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s26","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"The larger lesson for skill designers","t":"26 Section twenty-six Anthropic’s AI Governance Legal project is useful as a teaching example precisely because the interesting part is not the legal prose . The interesting part is the decomposition. Domain AI governance Jobs inventory · triage · assessment · vendor review · policy analysis · monitoring Individual skills trigger · workflow · branches · resources · outputs · boundaries · handoffs That is the transition from “write a good prompt about AI governance” to “design a reusable AI system for doing AI-governance work.” And that is what skill design really is."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s27","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"The forensic checklist","t":"27 Section twenty-seven When studying any skill — including the ones on this site — ask these questions. 1. Trigger What kinds of requests should activate it? 2. Job What single recognisable job does it own? 3. Inputs What information must exist before work begins? 4. References What knowledge should be consulted rather than baked into the skill? 5. Workflow What happens first, second, and third? 6. Branches Where can the workflow take different paths? 7. Stop conditions When should automatic processing stop? 8. Output contract What exactly should a successful result contain? 9. Boundary What is the skill specifically not authorised to do? 10. Handoff Which skill or human takes over next? 11. Configuration Which parts should change from organisation to organisation? 12. Platform dependencies What requires tools, data, integrations, or infrastructure beyond the skill itself? That last question is especially important. It prevents us from confusing a great skill with a complete product — and that distinction explains a great deal about the emerging legal AI market."},{"u":"case-study-anthropic-legal-skills.html#s28","p":"Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills","h":"The simplest way to remember all of this","t":"28 Section twenty-eight If the technical vocabulary starts to blur together, come back to this: YAML — what is this? Description — when should I use it? The SKILL.md body — how do I do the job? References — what information do I need? Scripts — what should code do reliably? Assets — what materials do I use in the output? Platform — where does all of this operate at scale? Find the right skill. Follow the right process. Consult the right information. Produce the right result. That is the core idea behind skill design."},{"u":"index.html","p":"AI Skills and agents","h":"AI Skills and agents","t":"An excerpt of the SLS AI Learning Hub Twenty-one AI skills built for law study — problem-first, policy-aware, and organised around verification. Download a whole set or a single skill, install it in ChatGPT or Claude, and see what happens when ten of them are loaded into one agent. Download a set How to install a skill Build the agent What is here Email the library library@law.stanford.edu SLS Tech Chat on Slack #techchat ↗ — open discussion about AI across the Law School. Check your course policy first. Whether AI is permitted for a particular assignment is set by your instructor, and the Honor Code applies either way. Access to Law Library–provided AI tools is limited to current SLS community members and uses Stanford sign-in."},{"u":"index.html","p":"AI Skills and agents","h":"AI Skills","t":"21 skills · 3 sets Every skill as its own download, three one-click sets, and five fictional practice drafts to run the writing skills on. Browse and download →"},{"u":"index.html","p":"AI Skills and agents","h":"Install a skill","t":"ChatGPT · Claude What a skill file actually is, and the steps to add one in either tool so it stays available across chats. Includes a printable guide. Read the steps →"},{"u":"index.html","p":"AI Skills and agents","h":"The Writing Partner agent","t":"Load the ten writing skills into one ChatGPT agent, so a draft is reviewed by all of them at once. Build the agent →"},{"u":"index.html","p":"AI Skills and agents","h":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","t":"The workshop packet: set-up, activities, discussion prompts, facilitator notes, and a glossary. Open the packet →"},{"u":"index.html","p":"AI Skills and agents","h":"How a skill is built","t":"Taking apart Anthropic’s open-source Claude for Legal project: what a SKILL.md is, and why the work is split into small skills. Read the case study →"},{"u":"install.html","p":"Install a skill","h":"Install a skill","t":"AI Skills and agents › Getting started Install a skill Each skill is a small ZIP file — a Markdown instruction file plus references. You never open it: download it once, upload it to ChatGPT or Claude, then work in plain English. You only add the ZIP once; after that the skill is available across chats. Browse the skills Build a ChatGPT agent instead Download the PDF guide If something goes wrong Troubleshooting The upload is rejected Check that you are uploading the ZIP itself, not a folder you extracted from it, and that the download completed. Re-download the file if in doubt. The skill installed but the assistant ignores it Name it in your first message — “use the Case Learning Coach skill” — and start a fresh chat. A skill added mid-conversation may not apply to that conversation. You cannot find where to add skills Both interfaces change often. If the menu does not match the recording above, email the Law Library and we will confirm the current path. You are not sure you should be using AI for this That is the prior question, and it has its own workflow. Work through the PAUSE Rule ↗ before installing anything. Access and policy. Access to Law Library–provided AI tools is limited to current SLS community members and uses Stanford sign-in. Confirm your course’s AI policy with the instructor. For access, tools, legal research, or technical assistance, email library@law.stanford.edu ."},{"u":"install.html#what-is-a-skill","p":"Install a skill","h":"What is a skill file?","t":"First, what it is A skill file is a set of written instructions that an AI assistant loads and follows. It is not a program and not a separate AI — it is a document that tells ChatGPT or Claude how to behave for a particular kind of work: what to ask you first, what steps to take in what order, what to refuse, and what to hand back. That is why these skills can be opinionated about law study. The instruction file is where the PAUSE Rule, the Honor Code reminder, the insistence on verifying authorities, and the refusal to write your brief for you all live. Keep the ZIP zipped. Both platforms want the packaged file. Unzipping it and uploading the contents is the most common reason an install does not work. A set is the one thing you do unzip. The skill sets are a single ZIP holding several skill ZIPs. Open the set once, then upload each skill file inside it — and leave those zipped."},{"u":"install.html#chatgpt","p":"Install a skill","h":"Install in ChatGPT","t":"For ChatGPT Download the skill ZIP. Keep it zipped. Add it to ChatGPT: click Plugins , toggle from Plugins to Skills , click the + icon, then drop the ZIP file into the upload box. Start with your goal in plain language, for example “Help me brief this case.”"},{"u":"install.html#claude","p":"Install a skill","h":"Install in Claude","t":"For Claude Download the skill ZIP. Do not unzip it. Add it to Claude: click Customize , then Add in the top right, and drag and drop the ZIP file into the upload box. Use natural language — start with your goal and ask Claude to use the skill."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"AI Skills","h":"AI Skills","t":"and agents › AI Skills For Stanford Law students AI Skills Twenty-one AI skills built for law study. Each one is problem-first, policy-aware, and organised around verification. Take a whole set in one download or a single skill on its own, then install it in ChatGPT or Claude — you add the ZIP once and it stays available across chats. Download a set How to install a skill Decide whether to use AI first → ↗ New · case study Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills Before you install one, see how a skill is actually built. We take apart Anthropic’s open-source Claude for Legal project — what a SKILL.md file is, why the work is split into small skills, and why an open skill does not make Harvey or Legora obsolete. Read the case study About 25 minutes · no coding background assumed. Or listen · audio explainer 21 min 55 sec The same ground as the written case study, spoken. Open in Google Drive ↗ For a class, a study group, or yourself Teach this: the Writing Partner packet A session built from what is on this page: set-up in ChatGPT and Claude, five activities to choose from, discussion topics with what to listen for, an explainer note on every skill, and a glossary of the jargon. Faculty can run it as it stands; on your own it is a structured way through the whole set. Open the packet 60 or 90 minutes · or at your own pace. What every skill has in common Shared principles The skills take a problem-first approach and build in the PAUSE Rule, Stanford Law School student AI guidance, Responsible AI at Stanford, productive struggle, legal-source verification, structured and manageable data, transparent AI-use logs, and accurate, non-anthropomorphic explanations of how AI systems work. Check your course policy first. Confirm your course’s AI policy with the instructor, and remember the Honor Code applies either way. Use Stanford’s own accounts — ChatGPT Edu ↗ and Claude ↗ — rather than a personal one. Access to Law Library–provided AI tools is limited to current SLS community members and uses Stanford sign-in. For access, tools, or research help, email library@law.stanford.edu ."},{"u":"skills.html#sets","p":"AI Skills","h":"Skill sets","t":"One download Take a whole set at once. A set is a single ZIP holding the skill files it names: unzip the set, then upload each skill ZIP without unzipping it. Every skill here works in both ChatGPT and Claude, and every one can also be downloaded on its own below."},{"u":"skills.html#sets","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Writing Partner Set","t":"10 skills New Ten skills that review your writing without writing it for you: the SLS policy gate, the full review workflow, and eight focused checks you can run on their own. Every one of them flags, explains, and asks — none of them drafts, rewrites, or replaces your prose. AI Use Gate Writing Review Argument & Structure Flow & Organization Clarity & Precision Audience & Reception Counterargument Claims & Sources Bluebook Audit Genre Fit Download the set See the ten skills Practice drafts Build a ChatGPT agent"},{"u":"skills.html#sets","p":"AI Skills","h":"Core Pathway Set","t":"5 skills The five foundational skills in order, from your first AI orientation to auditing AI output like a lawyer. Download the set See them"},{"u":"skills.html#sets","p":"AI Skills","h":"Tool Studios Set","t":"6 skills Every guided studio in one file — Harvey, Legora, LexText, CICERO, Gemini Notebook, and the explorer for anything not on the list. Download the set See them"},{"u":"skills.html#core","p":"AI Skills","h":"Core pathway","t":"Start here · in order Five foundational skills, from your first AI orientation to auditing AI output like a lawyer."},{"u":"skills.html#core","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS AI Orientation","t":"01 Core Start here: how AI actually works, the SLS student AI policy, the PAUSE Rule, and your first learning-and-disclosure record. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#core","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS AI Task-Fit Coach","t":"02 Core Decide whether AI is permitted, appropriate, and the right fit for a task — and get an AI Learning Plan with a clear go / no-go. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#core","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Case Learning Coach","t":"03 Core A Socratic partner for reading and briefing opinions. You attempt first; it questions, points to the text, and never writes your brief. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#core","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Legal Research Learning Coach","t":"04 Core A traditional-research-first workflow: validate authorities in authoritative sources, then use permitted AI to compare and stress-test. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#core","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS AI Verification Lab","t":"05 Core Audit AI output claim by claim — checking whether authorities exist, are current, and actually support the point. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"Writing partner","t":"For a draft you have written Ten skills for a draft you have already written. Each one reviews as a supervisor would: it identifies the problem, explains why it matters, points to the exact place, and hands the revision back to you. None of them drafts, rewrites, or supplies replacement prose. Start with the gate, then either run the full review or pick the check you need. No draft to try them on? Get a practice draft Load all ten into one ChatGPT agent →"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS AI Use Gate","t":"Gate Run this first. Works out whether AI review of this draft is authorised under your course policy or the SLS default — and flags confidentiality and professional-responsibility problems before anything reads the paper. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Writing Review","t":"Full review The whole workflow in one skill: the policy gate, then argument, organization, clarity, audience, counterarguments, sources, genre, and Bluebook — and a Word review copy you can work through. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Argument and Structure","t":"Focused check Tests the thesis, rule synthesis, evidence chain, and conclusion for gaps, then asks the revision questions instead of answering them. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Flow and Organization","t":"Focused check Whether the draft is ordered, signposted, and followable: sequencing, headings, transitions, repetition, and points buried where no reader will find them. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Clarity and Precision","t":"Focused check Ambiguity, jargon, dense sentences, and inconsistent defined terms, flagged where they sit — with revision strategies you carry out yourself. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Audience and Reception","t":"Focused check How a professor, judge, supervising lawyer, client, editor, or employer is likely to receive the draft: tone, credibility, framing, and unstated assumptions. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Counterargument Stress Test","t":"Focused check The strongest objections, missing adverse authority, and doctrinal soft spots — issue-spotting and questions only, never a rebuttal written for you. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Claims and Source Traceability","t":"Focused check Traces each claim to its source and separates “this source exists” from “this source supports the point,” ending in a claim-source matrix. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Bluebook Audit","t":"Focused check Bluebook 22nd-edition issue-spotting: short forms, pincites, signals, order, and typography. It names the likely defect and the rule; it does not write the citation. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Genre Fit","t":"Focused check Whether the draft behaves like the form it claims to be — memo, brief, seminar paper, policy paper, client letter, statement — on purpose, stance, sections, and conventions. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing-samples","p":"AI Skills","h":"Practice drafts","t":"Something to practise on Five fictional student drafts, so you can watch a review skill work before you hand it your own writing. Each one has real problems in it — that is the point. Download a draft, install a skill, upload the file, and ask for the review. Everything in these files is invented. The parties, the facts, the drafting, and the student authors are fictional, and the documents were written as teaching samples. Do not rely on any authority cited in them, and do not treat any of them as a model answer."},{"u":"skills.html#writing-samples","p":"AI Skills","h":"Daniels v. Metro Transit Authority","t":"Case brief Word · About 400 words A one-page brief of a negligence appeal: facts, procedural history, issue, rule, holding, reasoning, takeaway. The rule statement claims more than the case decides. Try it with Genre Fit · Argument and Structure · Clarity and Precision Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing-samples","p":"AI Skills","h":"Patel v. Green — false imprisonment","t":"Memo Word · About 700 words A short first-year memo with three headed elements and two authorities. The register slips towards the conversational, and the brief answer is more confident than the analysis under it. Try it with Clarity and Precision · Audience and Reception · Bluebook Audit Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing-samples","p":"AI Skills","h":"Lee v. Northstar Fitness — emotional distress","t":"Memo Word · About 1,000 words A longer memo working through the bystander elements one at a time, conceding the easy one and spending its argument on the contested one. Try it with Argument and Structure · Counterargument · Flow and Organization Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing-samples","p":"AI Skills","h":"Morgan v. Castillo — private facts","t":"Memo Word · About 1,200 words The most developed of the drafts, and the most heavily cited: six authorities, a quoted email, and a conclusion that has to weigh them. Try it with Claims and Source Traceability · Bluebook Audit · Counterargument Download"},{"u":"skills.html#writing-samples","p":"AI Skills","h":"Torts exam answer — the wet floor","t":"Exam Word · About 500 words A timed answer on negligence and a rescuer, written under exam conditions: elements marched through in order, no citations, conclusions ahead of the analysis. Try it with Argument and Structure · Flow and Organization · Audience and Reception Download"},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"Tool studios","t":"Explore when ready Guided, Stanford-specific introductions to the AI tools available through the Robert Crown Law Library and Stanford."},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Harvey Learning Studio","t":"06 Studio A problem-first, SLS-specific introduction to Harvey AI, with bounded practice and source verification. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Legora Learning Studio","t":"07 Studio Map your workflow, see where Legora helps, and practise on safe documents while verifying every source. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS LexText Learning Studio","t":"08 Studio Decide whether LexText fits a task, then learn a current feature through focused, safe practice. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS CICERO Oral Argument Studio","t":"09 Studio Prep an argument map and goals, run the simulation in CICERO, and debrief into a focused improvement plan. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS Gemini Notebook Learning Studio","t":"10 Studio Source-grounded study with a curated set of readings — inspect every cited passage instead of trusting it. Formerly NotebookLM. Download"},{"u":"skills.html#studios","p":"AI Skills","h":"SLS AI Tool Explorer","t":"11 Studio Bring any AI tool or feature and get a current, guided tutorial that starts from your problem. Download"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","t":"AI Skills and agents › AI Skills › Teach this Workshop packet · and a do-it-yourself path A session you can run — for a class, a study group, a journal, a clinic seminar, or a library workshop — on what these writing skills do, where their limits are, and who is responsible for the result. Everything it needs is already on this site. Work through it alone and it becomes a structured way to learn the whole set instead of poking at it. Start with set-up Jump to the activities Glossary of the jargon ↓ Runs in 60 or 90 minutes, or at your own pace Set-up before the session About 20 minutes, once, per person Everyone brings A laptop and their Stanford accounts Take what you need Five activities, five discussion sets — pick, reorder, cut This is a session about reviewing writing, not about producing it. Nobody should bring work they intend to submit unless the course that will receive it permits AI review, and no part of this packet authorises anything. Use the fictional practice drafts as the shared material: they exist so that a room can look at the same writing without anyone handing their graded work to an AI tool, or to each other. In this packet Accounts, first use the Stanford versions Set-up ten skills in ChatGPT, ten in Claude, one agent The practice drafts and what is deliberately wrong with them Five activities choose two or three Discussion topics with what to listen for Explainer notes each skill, and the case study Three ways to run it 60 minutes, 90 minutes, solo Glossary the jargon, in plain words Skill A set of written instructions an assistant loads and follows for a particular kind of work. Not a program and not a separate AI — a document that says what to ask, in what order, and what to refuse. SKILL.md The file at the heart of a skill. .md means Markdown: ordinary text with a little formatting, readable in any text editor. Markdown A way of writing formatted text in plain characters — # for a heading, - for a bullet, **bold** for bold. YAML frontmatter The small labelled block at the very top of a skill file, between two --- lines, holding its name and description. The label card on the outside of the folder. Metadata Information about something rather than the thing itself: a book’s author and year, a skill’s name and description. Description The sentence in the frontmatter that tells the assistant when this skill applies. It is what routes your request to the right skill, which is why a vague one fails quietly. References, scripts, assets The folders beside a skill file: knowledge it should consult, code for things that must be exact, and materials used in the final product (a Word template, say). Agent A named, reusable assistant you configure once — skills, standing instructions, attached files — and then talk to. The difference between a tool you re-explain every time and one that already knows the job. Instructions (or system instructions) The standing brief an agent carries into every conversation, as opposed to what you type in a single message. Prompt What you type. Everything else on this page is an attempt to stop the quality of the work depending on how well you happened to type it. Plugin A bundle of related skills, agents, and configuration distributed together — a practice area in a box. Connector A link between an assistant and a source of data — a document store, a research service, a firm system. Access, as distinct from instructions. Model The underlying system that generates text — Claude, GPT, and their relatives. A skill changes what the model is asked to do; it does not change what the model is. Large language model (LLM) The kind of system these are: trained to predict likely continuations of text. It is why the output is fluent, and why fluency is not evidence of accuracy. Context window How much text the assistant can hold in view at once. Exceed it and earlier material stops informing the answer — without any announcement that it has. Fabrication (“hallucination”) Confident output that is not true — an invented case, a real case that says something else, a quotation that was never written. The reason source verification is a separate step rather than a courtesy. Verification Opening the original and checking it yourself. Distinct from the assistant reporting that it found a source, which is not the same claim. Citator The legal service that tells you whether an authority is still good law. No web search substitutes for it when currentness matters, and no assistant has used one unless it says which. Claim–source matrix A table pairing each claim in a draft with the source meant to support it, and the verification status of that pairing. The output of the traceability skill. Tracked changes and comments Word’s markup. The review copy uses comments for diagnoses; every one of them is a proposal awaiting your decision, not an edit already made. Human review checkpoint A named place in a workflow where the machine stops and a person decides. The agent instructions define nine of them. Disclosure Telling the reader what AI assistance you used, in the form your course or journal requires. Required or not, it is the record that makes the rest defensible. Everything this packet uses The materials, in one place The ten skills and the practice drafts are on the skills page; the agent guide, the case study, and the PAUSE Rule each have their own. Nothing in this packet needs anything you cannot download in the next five minutes. The skills and the drafts Running this with a class? Email library@law.stanford.edu and we will help. ← The AI skills The case study → Robert Crown Law Library · last reviewed August 2026."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#accounts","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Use the Stanford versions of both tools","t":"1 Before anything else Sign in through Stanford, not with a personal account. The university versions are the ones covered by Stanford’s agreements and its guidance on what may be put into them; a personal free account is not, and what you paste into one is not governed by the same terms. Facilitators: confirm access a week out, not on the day. Getting an account provisioned is the single thing most likely to cost you the first fifteen minutes of a session, and it is the one thing nobody in the room can fix."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#accounts","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"ChatGPT Edu at Stanford","t":"OpenAI Stanford’s ChatGPT service, with university sign-in. This is where the skills and the agent go. uit.stanford.edu · ChatGPT Edu ↗"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#accounts","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Claude at Stanford","t":"Anthropic Stanford’s Claude service, with university sign-in. The same ten skill files install here. uit.stanford.edu · Claude ↗"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#setup","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Set-up: the same ten skills, three ways","t":"2 Do this first The point of installing in both tools and as an agent is not thoroughness for its own sake. It is that the three set-ups behave differently, and the differences are most of what there is to discuss. Ask participants to finish all three before the session; budget twenty minutes at the start if you would rather do it together. Everything below comes from one download: the SLS Writing Partner Set . Unzip that set once. Inside are ten skill files, each its own ZIP, and each of those stays zipped at every step that follows. A — The ten skills in ChatGPT In ChatGPT: Plugins , switch from Plugins to Skills , click + , and drop in each ZIP. Ten uploads, once. Full walkthrough with a recording: install a skill in ChatGPT . B — The ten skills in Claude In Claude: Customize , then Add at the top right, and drag each ZIP into the upload box. Same ten files, unchanged. Walkthrough: install a skill in Claude . C — One agent in ChatGPT holding all ten An agent is a named, reusable assistant that carries the skills, a standing set of instructions, and any files you attach — so you stop re-uploading and re-explaining in every chat. Follow Build a Writing Partner agent , which includes the instructions to paste and a short recording of the upload. Everyone is ready when They are signed in to both tools through Stanford. Ten skills are installed in ChatGPT, and ten in Claude — none of them unzipped first. One ChatGPT agent exists, named, with the instructions pasted in. At least one practice draft is downloaded and on the laptop. If you only have time for one: install the ten in whichever tool the group already uses, and demonstrate the other two from the front. The comparison activity needs two tools in the room, but it does not need two tools on every laptop — pairs work."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#drafts","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"The practice drafts are imperfect on purpose","t":"3 The shared material Five fictional student drafts sit on the skills page under Practice drafts : a case brief, three memos of increasing length and citation density, and a timed exam answer. They were written as teaching samples, and each one has real weaknesses built into it — that is what makes them useful. Nobody has to volunteer their own writing to have something worth reviewing in front of the room. Everything in them is invented — the parties, the facts, the drafting, and the student authors. No authority cited in them should be relied on, and none of them is a model answer. Say this out loud at the start; someone always asks whether Patel v. Green is real. Which draft to hand out depends on what you want the room to find: Draft What is weak in it Pairs well with Case brief Daniels, ~400 words The rule statement claims more than the case decides, and the takeaway drifts past the holding. Genre Fit, Argument and Structure Memo — false imprisonment Patel, ~700 words Conversational register, thin authority, and a brief answer more confident than the analysis under it. Clarity and Precision, Audience and Reception Memo — emotional distress Lee, ~1,000 words Elements handled one at a time, with the contested one carrying weight the reasoning does not. Counterargument, Flow and Organization Memo — private facts Morgan, ~1,200 words Six authorities and a quoted email — the richest target for source and citation work. Claims and Source Traceability, Bluebook Audit Torts exam answer The wet floor, ~500 words Conclusions arriving ahead of the analysis, and elements marched through in order regardless of weight. Argument and Structure, Audience and Reception A word of warning worth passing on. A group that reviews the same draft with the same skill will get five different reviews. That is not a malfunction, and noticing it is half the lesson — see the discussion topics below."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#activities","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Five activities","t":"Part two Choose two for an hour, three for ninety minutes. They are independent: any order, any combination. Each one names what it needs, what to do, what to watch for, and what to ask afterwards."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#activity-a","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Take a skill apart","t":"A 25–30 minutes · no laptop needed The question: what is a skill, and why does that matter to a lawyer? You need: the case study, Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills . Sections 5 to 14 are the core; section 27 is the checklist the activity uses. Read sections 5–14 in advance, or read section 6 and 7 aloud together (five minutes). In pairs, open any one of the ten writing skills you installed and answer four of the twelve forensic questions from section 27: what triggers it, what single job it owns, what it is not authorised to do, and who takes over next. Each pair reports the boundary they found — the thing their skill refuses to do. Watch for: the moment someone realises the file is just written instructions, not a separate intelligence. That realisation is what makes the rest of the session possible. Debrief: If a skill is a written procedure, whose procedure should it be — Anthropic’s, a vendor’s, the library’s, or your professor’s? What would a skill written by your instructor for your course say that these ten do not?"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#activity-b","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Same draft, same skill, two assistants","t":"B 25 minutes · both tools The question: how much of what comes back is the skill, and how much is the model running it? You need: one practice draft, the same skill installed in ChatGPT and in Claude, and an identical opening message in both. The Morgan memo with Claims and Source Traceability is the sharpest pairing; Bluebook Audit is a close second. Send the same file and the same request to both tools. Do not help either one. Put the two outputs side by side and mark, for each: what both found, what only one found, and anything either asserted that the other contradicted. Then check three of the findings against the draft itself. Which tool was right? Watch for: confident disagreement. Two assistants running identical written instructions on identical text will not produce identical findings, and neither output announces which one is wrong. Watch also for the tempting conclusion — that the longer review is the better one. Debrief: If two runs of the same skill disagree, what does that tell you about a single run you never compared? What would you have to do to know which is right, and how long would that take? Is the answer “use both every time” realistic?"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#activity-c","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Run the agent, then audit it","t":"C 30 minutes · the agent The question: what does a full review actually give you, and what does accepting it uncritically cost? You need: the ChatGPT agent from set-up C, and the Morgan or Lee memo. Hand the agent the draft and ask for a full review, including the Word review copy. Read the Human Review Required section it produces before reading anything else. Does it name what you would have named? Take three findings and rule on each: accept, decline, or revise differently — and write one sentence of reasoning for each ruling. Declining is a legitimate outcome; make someone in the room defend a decline. Take one flagged authority and try to verify it properly. Notice how long it takes. Watch for: the pull towards acceptance. An annotated Word file with tidy comments reads as authoritative, and clicking accept is faster than thinking. The exercise is designed to make that pull visible while the stakes are zero. Debrief: Whose writing is it after you accept twenty suggestions you did not evaluate? Where is the line between a reviewer improving your judgment and a reviewer replacing it?"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#activity-d","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Try to make it write for you","t":"D 15–20 minutes · the short one The question: where is the boundary, and how firm is it? Every one of these skills refuses the same thing: it will not draft, rewrite, or hand back replacement prose. This activity tests that claim rather than trusting it. Give a draft to any focused check and try, in three or four turns, to get it to write the fix for you. Ask for “an example of how that sentence could read.” Ask it to “show, not tell.” Say you are out of time. Record what it refused, what it conceded, and the exact wording that came closest to working. Compare across the room. The same pressure will not produce the same result twice. Watch for: the grey zone. A skill may legitimately offer a single word or a short phrase and still be a reviewer; a replacement paragraph is something else. Where the group puts that line is more interesting than where the tool puts it. Debrief: If you can talk a tool past its own boundary in four turns, what is the boundary actually for — and what is left holding the line? (Answer: the Honor Code, the course rule, and the person typing.) Would you disclose the turn that worked? Facilitator note. Frame this as a stress test of a tool, not as instruction in evasion. The finding you want is that a written boundary is a real constraint and a soft one, which is exactly why the rules that govern the student are not written in the file."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#activity-e","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Write the skill your course is missing","t":"E 30 minutes · the build The question: can you encode your own standard well enough that a machine could follow it? You need: the case study’s teaching template (there is a copy button on it), and a real standard from a course, journal, or clinic — a rubric, a style rule, a citation convention, a professor’s standing complaint. Copy the template and fill in the trigger and the description first: when should this run, and how would the assistant know? Write the workflow as numbered steps, then the output contract, then the boundary — what it must not do. Trade drafts with another group and run the twelve-question checklist against theirs. Watch for: how quickly “write well” turns out to be unwritable, and how much of legal writing instruction is tacit. Groups usually discover their standard has no stop condition and no boundary at all. Debrief: What part of your standard could not be written down? Is that because it is judgment, or because nobody has ever had to articulate it? Which answer should worry a teacher more?"},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#discussion","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Discussion topics","t":"Part three Five sets. Each has questions to put to the room and a note on what is worth listening for in the answers. None of them needs an activity to have been run first, though they land harder if one has."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#topic-authorship","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Authorship, and where it goes","t":"• Set one If a reviewer tells you a paragraph is unclear and you rewrite it, whose paragraph is it? What if the reviewer names the exact problem and you make the obvious fix? Is a human writing tutor doing something different in kind, or only in degree? At what point would you disclose the help, and to whom? Does it change if the reviewer is a classmate, a TA, a paid editor, or a skill? Would your answer change if the work were a journal note rather than a memo? Listen for: the slide from “it only diagnosed” to “so the revision is entirely mine.” Both halves can be true and the conclusion can still be too comfortable. Push on the case where the diagnosis was the insight."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#topic-verify","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Verification, and the cost of doing it properly","t":"• Set two What is the difference between a source that exists and a source that supports the point? How would you know if a quotation had been subtly altered? When does a public-web check stop being enough? What does a citator tell you that no assistant can? How long did verifying one authority take in activity C? What happens to that discipline at 2am the night before? Listen for: people treating “the assistant found the case” as the end of the process. The honest answer to the last question is the one worth having in the open: verification fails under time pressure, which is an argument about when to start, not about whether to verify."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#topic-what","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"What a skill is, and what it is not","t":"• Set three Does installing a skill make the assistant smarter, or only more specific? Why ten small skills instead of one large one? What could a skill never fix about the underlying model? If a firm encodes its house method as skills, what has it actually built? Who should be allowed to write the skills a law school distributes? What would you want to know before installing one from a stranger? Listen for: the assumption that a skill is a guarantee. A written procedure changes what the assistant attempts, not what it is capable of getting wrong."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#topic-tools","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Choosing the tool, and the shape of the market","t":"• Set four Where did the two assistants differ in activity B, and did it matter? What does a legal platform give you that a skill cannot? What does an open skill give you that a platform cannot? Which of these will you have access to after graduation? Whose data governance applies to what you paste in? What should a firm ask before buying either? Listen for: winner-takes-all framing. The case study’s stack diagram is the corrective: the interesting question is which layer you are choosing at, not which company wins."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#topic-not","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"When not to use any of this","t":"• Set five Which assignments should get no AI review at all, even where permitted? What are you practising when you find your own weak paragraph? What does a first-year lose by outsourcing the diagnosis? What would you never put into any of these tools, and why? How would you word an AI-use disclosure you would be comfortable signing? What would make you stop mid-review and close the window? Listen for: the difference between prohibited and unwise. Both matter, and only one of them is written down. The PAUSE Rule ↗ is the hub’s workflow for the second."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#notes","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Explainer notes","t":"Part four One note per skill: what it does, and the thing worth saying about it out loud. Use them as facilitator crib notes, as a handout, or as your own reading order."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#notes-skills","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"The writing partner skills","t":"• The ten SLS AI Use Gate Decides whether AI review of this draft is authorised at all, under the course rule or the SLS default, and flags confidentiality and professional-responsibility problems before anything reads the paper. Say out loud: this is the only skill in the set whose answer can be “stop.” A group that skips it has already made a decision without noticing. SLS Writing Review The umbrella workflow: the gate, then argument, organisation, clarity, audience, counterarguments, sources, genre, and Bluebook, ending in an annotated Word copy. Say out loud: it hands work to the nine others, so a set missing three of them quietly reviews less than it claims. SLS Argument and Structure Tests the thesis, rule synthesis, evidence chain, and conclusion for gaps, then asks the revision question rather than answering it. Say out loud: the questions it asks are the ones a supervising attorney asks. Reading them is training even when the diagnosis is wrong. SLS Flow and Organization Sequencing, headings, transitions, repetition, and the point buried where no reader will reach it. Say out loud: the easiest skill to agree with and the easiest to over-apply — a perfectly signposted argument can still be wrong. SLS Clarity and Precision Ambiguity, jargon, dense sentences, inconsistent defined terms, flagged in place with revision strategies rather than replacement sentences. Say out loud: this is where the reviewer/ghostwriter line gets tested hardest, because a “clearer version” is one request away. Activity D lives here. SLS Audience and Reception How a professor, judge, supervising lawyer, client, editor, or employer is likely to receive the draft: tone, credibility, framing, unstated assumptions. Say out loud: it is guessing at a reader it has never met. Its value is in making you specify the reader, which most drafts never do. SLS Counterargument Stress Test The strongest objections, missing adverse authority, doctrinal soft spots — issue-spotting and questions, never a rebuttal. Say out loud: the most useful of the ten for a strong writer, and the one whose output you are least able to check without doing the reading yourself. SLS Claims and Source Traceability Traces each claim to its source and separates “this source exists” from “this source supports the point,” ending in a claim–source matrix. Say out loud: the distinction it draws is the one that ends careers when it collapses. Pair it with the Morgan memo and make someone open an authority. SLS Bluebook Audit Bluebook 22nd-edition issue-spotting: short forms, pincites, signals, order, typography. It names the likely defect and the rule; it does not write the citation. Say out loud: it marks HUMAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED where the rule is uncertain or the source was never opened — and a confident citation correction from an assistant that never opened the source is exactly the failure mode to fear. SLS Genre Fit Whether the draft behaves like the form it claims to be — memo, brief, seminar paper, client letter — on purpose, stance, sections, and conventions. Say out loud: the best opener for a 1L room, because genre confusion is the most common and least-discussed failure in first-year writing."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#notes-case","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"The case study","t":"• The reading Reverse-engineering Anthropic’s AI governance legal skills takes apart a real, public, Apache-2.0 project — Anthropic’s Claude for Legal — and asks what a skill is made of. It is the reading that turns “I installed a thing” into “I know what I installed.” There is also a 21-minute audio explainer covering the same ground. It makes good pre-session listening for a group that will not do the reading, and it is the version to send anyone who would rather listen on a walk than sit with the diagrams. Sections 1–4 · skill or platform Why an open legal skill does not put Harvey or Legora out of business, and what the layers of the legal AI stack actually are. Use it for: discussion set four. Sections 5–14 · anatomy What a SKILL.md file contains, what YAML frontmatter is, and why the description is what routes work to the right skill. Use it for: activity A, and for anyone who thinks a skill is code. Sections 15–23 · design lessons Small skills over one large one; the file as a control panel rather than a warehouse; what belongs in references, scripts, and assets instead. Use it for: activity E. Sections 24–28 · the checklist Twelve questions to ask of any skill, ending with the one that matters most: what does this depend on that the file itself does not contain? Use it for: evaluating anything anyone hands you. Attribution to repeat. The case study is an independent educational reading of someone else’s open-source project. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by Anthropic, and its code samples are teaching illustrations rather than reproductions."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#plans","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Three ways to run it","t":"Part five Set-up happens before all three. If it cannot, add twenty minutes at the front and drop an activity."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"The short session","t":"Sixty minutes 5 min — what a skill is, and the practice drafts 25 min — activity B, two assistants 15 min — activity D, the boundary test 15 min — discussion sets one and two Leaves with: the tools disagree, and the boundary is soft."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"The full workshop","t":"Ninety minutes 10 min — set-up check and the drafts 25 min — activity A, take a skill apart 30 min — activity C, run the agent and audit it 25 min — discussion sets one, two, and five Leaves with: what it is, what it produces, and who is responsible."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"The DIY path","t":"On your own Set up all three, then read the case study Run activity B on the Morgan memo Run activity C, and verify one authority end to end Answer discussion set two in writing, for yourself Then, and only then, use it on your own draft Spread over a week, an hour at a time."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#adapt","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Adapting the packet","t":"• Make it yours Nothing here is fixed. Faculty are welcome to take the parts that fit a class and leave the rest; a study group can run one activity over lunch; a journal can use activity B and discussion set two alone and call it training. Three ways it bends: Swap the material. Any practice draft works with any skill; the table above is a suggestion, not a pairing rule. Swap the tool. Where a group has one platform, run everything there and demonstrate the other. Add your standard. Attach your own rubric or style guide to the agent and the review changes with it. Narrow it. A single skill, a single draft, and one discussion set is a legitimate twenty-minute session. Print it. This page is written to print cleanly as a handout. Ask us. The library will run it with your class if you would rather not. One thing not to adapt away: the course-policy question. Whatever else you cut, keep the point that permission comes first and that no session, packet, or tool grants it."},{"u":"teach-this-writing-partner.html#glossary","p":"Teach this: the Writing Partner","h":"Glossary","t":"Part six The words that come up in this packet, in plain English. Most of them sound more technical than they are."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","t":"AI Skills and agents › AI Skills › Writing Partner agent Set-up guide · ChatGPT Installed one at a time, the ten writing skills are ten separate uploads you repeat in every new chat. Loaded into a ChatGPT agent , they become one reviewer you name, brief once, and hand a draft to — with your syllabus and style guide attached, and the same human-review rules every time. Start the set-up Get the ten skills Jump to the instructions to copy ↓ Do not build this for a course that does not permit AI use. Check your course’s AI policy before you start, and confirm with the instructor if it is not clear. A course that prohibits AI assistance prohibits this, and the Honor Code applies either way. If your course does permit it but requires disclosure, keep a record of what you used and when — and work through the PAUSE Rule ↗ if you are not sure whether to use AI on this piece of work at all. What you need A ChatGPT account with Agents in the sidebar What you install The ten writing partner skill ZIPs How long About ten minutes, once Official documentation Workspace agents in ChatGPT ↗ From OpenAI The official documentation Agents are ChatGPT’s feature, not ours, and the interface changes. If a button has moved or a menu does not match this guide, OpenAI’s announcement is the current word on what agents are and what they can hold. Workspace agents in ChatGPT ↗ Still stuck? Email library@law.stanford.edu . ← The AI skills Installing a single skill → Robert Crown Law Library · last reviewed August 2026."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#steps","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Set it up","t":"Seven steps You do this once per course. Everything below happens inside ChatGPT."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-1","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Download the ten skills","t":"1 First Take the SLS Writing Partner Set in one download, then unzip that set once. Inside are the ten skill files, each its own ZIP. Keep every skill ZIP zipped. You unzip the set , and nothing else. Each skill goes up to ChatGPT as the packaged ZIP file it arrived as — unzipping one and uploading its contents is the most common reason an install fails."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-2","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Create a blank agent","t":"2 In ChatGPT In the ChatGPT sidebar, find Agents and click the + beside it — or open Browse agents and choose Create agent . ChatGPT offers to build an agent from a description. Ignore the suggestions and choose Start blank in the top right: you are bringing your own skills and instructions."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-3","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Give the agent a name","t":"3 Name it Type a name over the placeholder at the top of the editor. Name it for the work it does, not for the tool — you will end up with more than one of these, and “Agent 2” will not tell you which is which in November. Writing Partner — Torts Writing Partner — LRW memo Writing Partner — Seminar paper Writing Partner — Journal note"},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-4","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Add the ten skill files","t":"4 The skills Under the name you just typed there is a row of buttons: Browse apps , Add skill , Upload files , Memory . Click Add skill and add each of the ten ZIPs, still zipped. The recording below shows the upload. Your browser cannot play this recording. Download the video instead. Adding files to an agent. Fifteen seconds, no sound. Order does not matter, but all ten do. The full Writing Review hands work to the focused checks, so a set missing three of them quietly reviews less than it says it does. If you only ever want one kind of review — Bluebook, say — build a smaller agent on purpose rather than by accident."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-5","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Paste in the instructions","t":"5 The brief The Instructions box is where you tell the agent how to behave. Copy the text below into it whole. It does two things: it points the agent at the skills you just installed, and it holds the line that makes these skills worth using — the agent diagnoses, and you decide. Read it before you paste it. You are the one who has to live with what it says. Instructions for the agent # Role You are a writing review partner for a Stanford Law School student. Work only through the SLS writing skills installed on this agent: the AI Use Gate, the full Writing Review, and the focused checks for argument and structure, flow and organisation, clarity and precision, audience and reception, counterargument, claims and source traceability, Bluebook, and genre fit. Choose the skill that fits what the student asks for. Run the AI Use Gate first whenever the work may be submitted, graded, published, used in a clinic, or otherwise evaluated. # Non-negotiable boundary Act as a reviewer, not a ghostwriter. Identify the problem, explain why it matters, point to the exact place, and hand the revision back to the student. Do not draft, rewrite, or silently replace the student's prose, and do not supply a polished substitute sentence or paragraph. # Human Review Checkpoints Human judgment is a required part of this workflow. The agent may identify problems, gather evidence, test reasoning, verify sources, and explain options. It must not convert those findings into final substantive decisions for the student. Use the following checkpoints in every applicable review. ## Checkpoint 1 — Permission and scope Before reviewing work that may be submitted, graded, published, used in a clinic, or otherwise evaluated: - determine whether the requested AI use is permitted; - identify any course-, instructor-, clinic-, journal-, employer-, or assignment-specific rule; - ask the student for the applicable rule when it cannot be reliably determined; - stop any prohibited form of assistance. Do not proceed on the assumption that because the technology can perform a task, the student is permitted to use it for that task. Human decision: the student is responsible for determining that the intended use is permitted and for obtaining authorization when required. ## Checkpoint 2 — Purpose and audience Before making recommendations that depend heavily on purpose, genre, or audience, determine whether the intended context is sufficiently clear. Examples include: - predictive memorandum versus persuasive brief; - seminar paper versus client communication; - professor versus judge; - scholarly audience versus general audience. If the document itself makes the context clear, proceed. If a materially different review would result depending on the answer, ask one focused question. Human decision: the student determines the actual purpose, audience, and constraints of the writing. ## Checkpoint 3 — Diagnostic review before revision The agent may identify weaknesses in thesis, reasoning, organization, flow, clarity, audience reception, counterarguments, sourcing, and citation form. But the agent must distinguish between: - a diagnostic finding — something appears weak, unsupported, confusing, incomplete, or vulnerable; and - a revision decision — how the paper should ultimately change. The first is appropriate for the agent. The second belongs to the student. For significant issues, frame the feedback so the student must make the substantive choice. For example: \"Your conclusion depends on treating X as established. The cited authority clearly supports A, but I could not verify B. Decide whether the proposition should be narrowed, additional authority is needed, or your reasoning supports B independently.\" \"These two sections appear to make overlapping arguments. Consider whether they perform different analytical functions before deciding whether to combine, separate, or reorder them.\" Do not make that decision for the student. ## Checkpoint 4 — Source verification The agent should perform rigorous source traceability, including web research when available. For consequential claims, distinguish: - source located; - source verified; - proposition supported; - proposition only partially supported; - quotation verified; - pinpoint verified; - legal currentness independently verified; - unable to verify. Finding an apparent source is not the end of the process. Whenever the agent finds a source mismatch, questionable quotation, unsupported proposition, conflicting authority, missing primary authority, a source that supports only part of a claim, or legal authority whose current status has not been checked, flag the issue for human review. Human decision: the student must open and review consequential authorities before relying on them in the final paper. For legal authorities, remind the student that a public-web source check is not a substitute for an appropriate legal citator when currentness matters. Do not state that an authority has been Shepardized, KeyCited, or otherwise citator-checked unless that service was actually used. ## Checkpoint 5 — Argument and counterargument The agent may identify an inferential gap, missing premise, counterargument, alternative interpretation, adverse authority, limiting principle, or unsupported assumption. Do not fill the gap by writing the missing analysis. Instead, identify the problem and present the analytical question the student needs to resolve. For example: \"A skeptical reader could argue that the court's reasoning turns on X rather than Y. Your current analysis does not appear to address that interpretation. Decide whether the distinction matters and, if so, what evidence or reasoning supports your position.\" Human decision: the student decides what argument to make and how to answer competing arguments. ## Checkpoint 6 — Major structural recommendations Treat significant structural changes as recommendations rather than automatic corrections. Examples include moving an entire section, dividing or consolidating major arguments, changing the thesis, eliminating a line of analysis, changing the order of legal issues, and reframing the document for another audience. Flag the recommendation and explain the reason. Do not automatically implement the change in the student's document. Human decision: the student chooses whether to make major organizational or substantive changes. ## Checkpoint 7 — Bluebook and citation review The agent may identify likely citation problems and explain the relevant rule or category. The student must make the final citation correction. For significant or uncertain issues, mark HUMAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED when: - the applicable Bluebook rule is uncertain; - multiple citation forms may be defensible; - journal or instructor rules may supersede the default; - the source itself has not been opened; - the pinpoint cannot be verified; - the status of an authority requires a citator. Do not manufacture a definitive correction merely to produce an answer. Human decision: the student verifies the citation against the applicable Bluebook edition and any controlling local rules. ## Checkpoint 8 — Before producing the Word review copy Before generating the final annotated Word review document, confirm internally that: - the student's original prose has not been rewritten; - comments contain diagnoses, questions, and suggestions rather than replacement prose; - substantive changes have not been silently implemented; - source findings are accurately labeled by confidence and verification status; - unresolved questions remain unresolved rather than being filled by inference. The Word review copy should make the student responsible for the revision. Do not accept or implement substantive changes on the student's behalf. ## Checkpoint 9 — Student review of AI findings At the end of a full review, give the student a short \"Human Review Required\" section identifying the most important items the student personally needs to inspect before revising. Use these categories: - Verify personally — sources, quotations, legal authorities, pincites, factual claims, and Bluebook issues that require inspection of an original source. - Decide — argument, thesis, organization, audience, counterargument, or framing questions that require student judgment. - Revise yourself — passages where the agent has identified a problem but intentionally has not supplied replacement prose. - Confirm compliance — any required AI disclosure, instructor permission, course restriction, clinic requirement, journal rule, or other applicable condition. Keep this section short and prioritized. # Required final human checkpoint Never describe the reviewed paper as correct, finished, Bluebook compliant, fully verified, ready to submit, or legally authoritative solely because the agent completed its review. Instead, conclude a comprehensive review with language substantially like: \"Human review still required: I identified and investigated issues in the draft, but you remain responsible for the argument, revisions, source verification, citation accuracy, and compliance with the rules governing this work. Review the flagged authorities and quotations against the originals, use an appropriate legal citator where currentness matters, make the substantive revisions yourself, and confirm any required AI-use disclosure or authorization before submission.\" The purpose of these checkpoints is not merely to catch AI mistakes. They preserve the student's authorship, judgment, research responsibility, and learning throughout the writing process. Prefer a file? Download the same text as Markdown . Scroll the box to read it all. Add your own rules underneath. If your instructor requires a particular citation format, bans a kind of assistance, or wants AI use disclosed in a set form, write that at the bottom of the instructions in your own words. Your course’s rule governs, and an agent cannot follow a rule it was never told."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-6","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Attach your syllabus or style guide","t":"6 Optional, and worth it Use Upload files for the documents that define what good looks like in this course. The agent can then check your draft against the actual assignment instead of a generic idea of one. The course syllabus The assignment sheet or prompt A style guide or writing handbook Your instructor’s AI policy, in full A journal’s house citation rules A rubric, if you were given one Do not upload anything confidential. Client material, clinic files, unpublished work belonging to someone else, and anything covered by a confidentiality obligation stay off the agent unless your supervising lawyer or instructor has approved that use and the account is an approved one. Access to Law Library–provided AI tools is limited to current SLS community members and uses Stanford sign-in."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#step-7","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Create it, then test it on something that is not yours","t":"7 Finish Click Create at the top of the editor. Preview opens a chat with the agent so you can try it before you rely on it. Hand it one of the practice drafts — fictional student writing with real problems in it — and ask for a full review. Read what comes back with the question “would I have caught this?” in mind. If the agent rewrites your sentences instead of flagging them, the instructions did not paste cleanly. You are set up when The agent has a name you will recognise in three months. All ten skills are listed on it, and none was unzipped first. The instructions are in the Instructions box, plus anything your course requires. A practice draft comes back with flags and questions — not with your prose rewritten."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#per-course","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"A separate agent for each course you write for","t":"• Build more than one Courses do not share a rulebook. One professor permits AI review of drafts, another prohibits it entirely, a journal has its own citation conventions, and a clinic adds confidentiality obligations that no seminar has. An agent carrying the wrong course’s syllabus and the wrong course’s AI policy will give you confident advice under rules that do not apply to your work. So build one per course, name it for that course, and attach that course’s materials. The skills are the same in each; the rules and the documents are not. When a course ends, delete its agent rather than repurposing it — that is how the wrong syllabus survives into next term."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#verify","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"Verify the output. Never take it on trust","t":"• Every time An agent that has been given careful instructions still produces confident, fluent, and occasionally wrong work. Fluency is not accuracy, and a well-formatted review is not a verified one. Treat everything that comes back as a set of claims to check, not findings to accept. Read every comment. Decide on every one. An accepted change you did not think about is still your writing, and still your responsibility. When the review comes back as an annotated Word copy, work through it change by change and comment by comment: Open the flagged authority yourself. Check the quotation against the original. Check the pincite on the page, not in the summary. Use a citator when currentness matters. Decide whether the diagnosis is right. Accept, decline, or revise it your own way. Keep the reasoning you can defend. Reject the change you cannot explain. Declining a suggestion is a legitimate outcome and often the correct one. The agent has read your draft; it has not sat in your class, read the assignment the way your instructor meant it, or heard what the professor said about the point in week six. You have. The work stays yours. You remain responsible for the argument, the revisions, source verification, citation accuracy, and compliance with whatever rules govern the assignment — including any required disclosure of AI use. A review by an agent changes none of that."},{"u":"writing-partner-agent.html#checkpoints","p":"Build a Writing Partner agent in ChatGPT","h":"The nine human-review checkpoints","t":"• What you pasted, in short The instructions above put nine checkpoints into every review. Each one marks a place where the agent stops and a human decides. This is the summary; the full text is in the box you copied. 1. Permission and scope Is this AI use permitted here at all? The agent asks for the rule when it cannot tell, and stops any prohibited assistance. You confirm the use is authorised. 2. Purpose and audience A predictive memo is not a brief and a professor is not a judge. If the answer would change the review, the agent asks one focused question. You define the real purpose and audience. 3. Diagnosis before revision The agent separates “this looks weak” from “here is how the paper should change.” The first is its job. You make the second decision. 4. Source verification Locating a source is not verifying it, and verifying it is not the same as its supporting the proposition. The agent labels which it did. You open the consequential authorities. 5. Argument and counterargument The agent names the gap, the adverse authority, the objection a sceptical reader would raise — and does not write the answer. You decide what argument to make. 6. Structural recommendations Moving a section, changing the thesis, cutting a line of analysis: recommendations with reasons, never silent edits. You choose whether to restructure. 7. Bluebook and citation Likely defects and the rule behind them, marked HUMAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED where the rule is uncertain or the source was never opened. You make the correction. 8. Before the Word review copy The agent confirms it has not rewritten your prose, implemented substantive changes, or filled an unresolved question with inference. 9. Human Review Required Every full review ends with a short, prioritised list: what to verify personally, what to decide, what to revise yourself, and what compliance to confirm. And a rule that outranks all nine: the agent never calls your paper correct, finished, Bluebook compliant, fully verified, or ready to submit merely because it finished reviewing."}];
